{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-20746363-review.md", "content": "# Session review: compact-output task with varied demonstrations

This session had a test grid partitioned by separator rows/columns, while the demonstrations varied widely in size, palette, and output dimensions. The solver did not establish a consistent tile-to-output correspondence or exact encoding from any demo. It guessed constant/compact patterns, submitted a repeated 4x2 candidate after it was rejected, and requested several more demonstrations without adequately integrating their evidence. Those actions produced incorrect answers and unnecessary cost.

Reusable lesson: for compact partitioned outputs, explicitly enumerate panels, separators, output shape, marker coordinates/counts, and output values for every demo. Keep dimensions, correspondence, selector, and encoding as independent hypotheses. A changed palette/scale across demos may still admit an abstract shared mapping, but do not infer one from appearance. Replay any proposed rule exactly across every complete demo before submitting. After a wrong submission, discard the entire failed rule; never repeat the same grid. Request a demo only when two explicit live hypotheses predict different, answer-relevant results. Maintain the Continual-ARC contract: exactly one legal JSON action on the final line on every turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u079/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-20746363-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-inference.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis from heterogeneous demos

When a demo appears unrelated to another by palette, dimensions, or local geometry, first inspect whether each input nevertheless encodes an ordered sequence or lattice that is expanded into a full periodic output. For a periodic candidate, independently derive the period, symbol/color order, direction, and phase/origin from the input markers and output. Verify the complete generated grid against every demo, including row/column transitions; a visually plausible periodic fill is not evidence by itself. Heterogeneity alone is not grounds either to dismiss a shared abstract rule or to repeatedly request demonstrations without a discriminating question.

This is a diagnostic technique, not a solved task-specific mapping. In the reviewed stream, the demos included sparse color tracks and an already periodic output; the test likewise contained diagonal color tracks, but no transfer rule was rigorously established before the next demo request. Future analyses should extract and validate the proposed sequence-to-field mapping before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u079/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-inference.md"}
